{"name":"Shark Loop","hex":"#a5b4ba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any predatory fish of the superorder Selachimorpha, with a cartilaginous skeleton and 5 to 7 gill slits on each side of its head.","Meat of this animal, consumed as food."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fish for sharks.","Of a university student who is not a fresher (first-year undergraduate), and especially towards the start of the academic year:\n To engage in sexual activity with a fresher.\n To cruise for casual sex with a fresher at a bar or club."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shark"},{"term":"loop","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A length of thread, line or rope that is doubled over to make an opening.","The opening so formed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form something into a loop.","To fasten or encircle something with a loop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/loop"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg/500px-Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg/1280px-Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg/500px-Fossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil shark tooth (size over 9 cm or 3.5 inches) with crown, shoulder, root and root lobe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%2BFossiler_Haifischzahn_-_Gr%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_%C3%BCber_9_cm_-_mit_Krone_-_Schulter_-_Wurzel_und_Wurzellappen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG/500px-CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG/1280px-CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CretaceousShar